To demonstrate, let's load the `vasp` module and then use the `module list`
command to show which modules we currently have loaded in our environment.
([VASP](https://www.vasp.at/) is parallel materials science code
designed for high-performance simulation of electronic structure.)

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module load vasp
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module list
```
{: .language-bash}

```
Currently Loaded Modules:
  1) craype-x86-rome                         6) bolt/0.8          11) cray-dsmml/0.2.2       16) cray-hdf5-parallel/1.12.2.1
  2) libfabric/1.12.1.2.2.0.0                7) epcc-setup-env    12) cray-mpich/8.1.23      17) vasp/6/6.4.3
  3) craype-network-ofi                      8) load-epcc-module  13) PrgEnv-gnu/8.3.3
  4) perftools-base/22.12.0                  9) gcc/11.2.0        14) cray-libsci/22.12.1.1
  5) xpmem/2.5.2-2.4_3.30__gd0f7936.shasta  10) craype/2.7.19     15) cray-fftw/3.3.10.3
```
{: .output}

So in this case, loading the `vasp` module also loaded a variety of other
modules. Let's try unloading the `namd` package.

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module unload vasp
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module list
```
{: .language-bash}

```
Currently Loaded Modules:
  1) craype-x86-rome            4) perftools-base/22.12.0                  7) epcc-setup-env
  2) libfabric/1.12.1.2.2.0.0   5) xpmem/2.5.2-2.4_3.30__gd0f7936.shasta   8) load-epcc-module
  3) craype-network-ofi         6) bolt/0.8                                9) gcc/11.2.0
```
{: .output}

So using `module unload` "un-loads" a module along with its dependencies.
